WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.196 --> 00:00:01.029
- He's an American, former,
major league short stop

00:00:01.029 --> 00:00:05.112
who, played 20 seasons
with the New York Yankees.

00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:08.284
He's won five World Series championships

00:00:08.284 --> 00:00:09.967
and is considered one
of the greatest players

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:11.261
of his generation.

00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:13.799
He's finished six all time and career hits

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:15.670
and first among short stops.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.080
He's Derek Jeter.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.663
And here are his top
ten rules for success.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.621
- One thing I've always
prioritized you know,

00:00:26.621 --> 00:00:28.454
my number one priortiy

00:00:30.123 --> 00:00:33.290
for the last 20 years,
was my baseball career.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.542
That's it.

00:00:34.542 --> 00:00:36.124
I did a lot of other things,

00:00:36.124 --> 00:00:39.240
but I never waivered from
that being my number one goal.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.324
I'm always diggin', setting goals

00:00:41.324 --> 00:00:43.017
and trying to reach those goals

00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:44.885
and working hard

00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:46.551
and there's steps along the way

00:00:46.551 --> 00:00:51.184
so I think prioritizing
is the number one thing.

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:54.060
Sometimes people have
so many different goals

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.892
that go in this direction,
that direction and they

00:00:55.892 --> 00:00:59.558
never reach the potential
of one particular avenue

00:00:59.558 --> 00:01:04.219
that they're going so, I
mean throughout my career

00:01:04.219 --> 00:01:06.807
I've been very involved
in different businesses

00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:10.098
and marketing and foundation
but my number one priority

00:01:10.098 --> 00:01:14.430
is always the game and now
my priority's changed so

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.180
I can focus on some other things.

00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:20.960
- [Announcer] Popped up
short center, Jeter out.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.920
Canole and did Jeter catch it?

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.005
Wow, what a miraculous catch!

00:01:26.005 --> 00:01:27.263
(crowd cheering)

00:01:27.263 --> 00:01:28.263
unbelievable.

00:01:28.263 --> 00:01:31.895
- I've always focused on work ethic.

00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:35.202
You know I've always been
taught there's always going to

00:01:35.202 --> 00:01:37.321
be someone that's better,
but there's no reason

00:01:37.321 --> 00:01:38.779
why someone should outwork you.

00:01:38.779 --> 00:01:40.573
And I think that applies not
only to sports but it applies

00:01:40.573 --> 00:01:41.909
to anything that you try to do.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:44.875
You have to work harder than
other people if you want

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:46.125
to be the best.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:49.987
Never be satisfied.

00:01:49.987 --> 00:01:52.905
You know I am never satisfied
I always try to think of

00:01:52.905 --> 00:01:55.738
what I could do next to be better.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.190
- [Announcer] Giel into
left field well hit.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:06.357
Track, wall, off the top of
the wall into third is Perez,

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:10.939
they bring him around,
throw to the plate by Jeter,

00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:13.400
quick throw into Novo.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.438
- You have to win here,
that's the bottom line is

00:02:16.438 --> 00:02:17.972
what have you done lately?

00:02:17.972 --> 00:02:20.180
You know we were able to
accomplish a lot of good things

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.523
early on, we've been post
season every year so far but

00:02:23.523 --> 00:02:26.519
the bottom line is when you're
competing you want to win.

00:02:26.519 --> 00:02:28.803
I mean that's why you're out there.

00:02:28.803 --> 00:02:30.757
Finishing second, finishing
third, going to the

00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:32.383
post season's not good enough.

00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:34.672
I think everyone should
have that mentality,

00:02:34.672 --> 00:02:36.588
I mean you go into a season,
you win a championship,

00:02:36.588 --> 00:02:38.090
or it doesn't mean anything.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.509
- Have you always had
that kind of mentality,

00:02:39.509 --> 00:02:41.255
even before you got to New York?

00:02:41.255 --> 00:02:42.425
Or did it
- No question, no I was younger

00:02:42.425 --> 00:02:44.467
I always wanted, my
daddy used to beat me at

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:45.759
everything we did.

00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:47.131
- [Interviewer] He wouldn't let you win?

00:02:47.131 --> 00:02:48.251
- No, no he wouldn't let
me win so now I don't let

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:49.788
anyone else win.

00:02:49.788 --> 00:02:53.705
So I've always had that
mentality, it's pointless

00:02:53.705 --> 00:02:58.243
from my point of view if you're
competing and you don't win.

00:02:58.243 --> 00:03:00.993
(crowd cheering)

00:03:01.863 --> 00:03:03.115
- [Announcer] And the two oh.

00:03:03.115 --> 00:03:04.613
There it is!

00:03:04.613 --> 00:03:06.072
(crowd cheering)

00:03:06.072 --> 00:03:07.784
for 72 years, Lou Gherig has
been the Yankee's all time

00:03:07.784 --> 00:03:10.617
hits leader, now it's Derek Jeter.

00:03:14.407 --> 00:03:16.695
- Family they're the ones that,
I have a very close family.

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:20.862
They've always been very
supportive, more importantly

00:03:22.274 --> 00:03:25.234
they've always been very honest.

00:03:25.234 --> 00:03:29.654
I think a lot of times people
in general like to surround

00:03:29.654 --> 00:03:31.618
themselves with people that
are going to tell them that

00:03:31.618 --> 00:03:33.041
everything they're doing is right.

00:03:33.041 --> 00:03:35.749
You know what I mean, you
need people around you

00:03:35.749 --> 00:03:37.377
that are going to tell
you you're doing wrong.

00:03:37.377 --> 00:03:40.418
And my parents are very very
honest, even if I don't want

00:03:40.418 --> 00:03:42.284
to hear something, they'll
tell me, they'll give me

00:03:42.284 --> 00:03:46.376
their opinion so I'd say
family is most important and

00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:48.374
then secondly your friends.

00:03:48.374 --> 00:03:52.333
It's the same thing I think
your friends are the ones

00:03:52.333 --> 00:03:55.583
that are going to help ya,
they're the ones that are going to

00:03:55.583 --> 00:03:58.119
support you, at times they're
going to be the ones that

00:03:58.119 --> 00:04:02.079
get you in trouble, so
you have to make sure that

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:03.794
you surround yourself with
a good group of friends.

00:04:03.794 --> 00:04:07.961
- [Announcer] And no one long,
a high pop, off third foul,

00:04:09.464 --> 00:04:11.435
(inaudible) and Jeter
there, Jeter makes the catch

00:04:11.435 --> 00:04:13.602
and falls into the crowd.

00:04:13.602 --> 00:04:14.685
And he's out.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.808
They gave him the out call
and Grosser now throws

00:04:18.808 --> 00:04:23.618
to second, and now time is
called, and I don't know

00:04:23.618 --> 00:04:24.962
what the ruling is.

00:04:24.962 --> 00:04:29.129
Chavez went to second 'cause
Jeter was in the crowd.

00:04:30.377 --> 00:04:33.589
And Jeter now climbs out of
the crowd, but Derek Jeter

00:04:33.589 --> 00:04:37.461
made an unbelievable catch
and as he hit with his waist

00:04:37.461 --> 00:04:41.628
on the railing down the third
base line, he back flipped

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.784
into the crowd and held
onto the ball, what a catch

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:47.404
by Derek Jeter.

00:04:47.404 --> 00:04:48.695
What a play.

00:04:48.695 --> 00:04:52.445
- I learned something from
watching Mr. Torrey for all

00:04:52.445 --> 00:04:54.359
these years, you know you
always hear the phrase you

00:04:54.359 --> 00:04:56.317
treat everyone the same,
well that's not correct,

00:04:56.317 --> 00:04:57.812
you treat everyone fairly.

00:04:57.812 --> 00:04:59.936
You try to be the same every
day, you don't come and

00:04:59.936 --> 00:05:02.478
hold your head up high when
things are going well and

00:05:02.478 --> 00:05:04.480
things are going bad
you find a way to hide.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.811
Leadership I don't think is
something that's appointed,

00:05:06.811 --> 00:05:08.845
I think it's something that's earned.

00:05:08.845 --> 00:05:10.511
(crowd cheering)

00:05:10.511 --> 00:05:13.261
- [Announcer] Jeter hits it
to right, back at the wall,

00:05:13.261 --> 00:05:16.856
It's over, Yankees win
and the series is tied.

00:05:16.856 --> 00:05:19.988
I have a lot of confidence,
I think you have to have

00:05:19.988 --> 00:05:21.528
confidence in your ability.

00:05:21.528 --> 00:05:25.652
The stronger you are
mentally I think the better

00:05:25.652 --> 00:05:29.403
you're going to perform so
I've always had confidence

00:05:29.403 --> 00:05:32.273
any time I'm in a situation
I tend to look back and

00:05:32.273 --> 00:05:36.070
think back to times that
I had success and I think

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.730
that's helped me out throughout.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:42.607
("Empire State of Mind" - Jay-Z)

00:05:42.607 --> 00:05:44.059
- [Announcer] And toward the
hole, Jeter backhands from

00:05:44.059 --> 00:05:45.057
the outfield.

00:05:45.057 --> 00:05:47.807
(crowd cheering)

00:05:48.763 --> 00:05:50.096
Man what a play!

00:05:51.443 --> 00:05:53.577
- I'm fans of athletes from
all different sports, I've

00:05:53.577 --> 00:05:56.171
never tried to emulate anyone else.

00:05:56.171 --> 00:05:57.879
I've tried to be myself.

00:05:57.879 --> 00:06:00.794
I've learned a lot of
watching guys, especially guys

00:06:00.794 --> 00:06:02.170
when I was younger.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:06.298
But I think in terms of
leadership, I think leadership

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:09.091
is viewed differently by
different people, I mean there's

00:06:09.091 --> 00:06:13.258
people who lead by example,
there's people that are vocal

00:06:14.301 --> 00:06:17.626
leaders, people that do
a little bit of both.

00:06:17.626 --> 00:06:20.584
I think you just have to be who you are.

00:06:20.584 --> 00:06:22.248
- How would you categorize
your leadership?

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:24.415
- Uh a little bit of both.

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:30.717
I really try to take the time
to get to know the people

00:06:30.717 --> 00:06:32.631
that I'm leading, I think
that's most important.

00:06:32.631 --> 00:06:37.048
I don't think you walk around
as a self proclaimed leader.

00:06:37.048 --> 00:06:41.208
I think if you do things
the right way, you're honest

00:06:41.208 --> 00:06:43.916
with people, people may
tend to follow but I don't

00:06:43.916 --> 00:06:48.625
put on a show I don't do
things in front of the camera.

00:06:48.625 --> 00:06:53.337
I've had plenty of conversations
with teammates behind

00:06:53.337 --> 00:06:55.878
closed doors, you know I've
always heard you're not vocal,

00:06:55.878 --> 00:06:58.628
he's just lead by example but
how would people know unless

00:06:58.628 --> 00:07:01.128
they were behind closed doors?

00:07:02.844 --> 00:07:05.471
So I have no problem with
that but like I said I think

00:07:05.471 --> 00:07:09.688
the most important aspect
of leadership is getting

00:07:09.688 --> 00:07:11.724
to know the people that you're leading.

00:07:11.724 --> 00:07:14.340
- [Announcer] (inaudible) you're
not going to run you're not

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.550
going to hit, you got
to wait for a gapper.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.294
(crowd cheering)

00:07:18.294 --> 00:07:19.881
down the right field line,

00:07:19.881 --> 00:07:23.303
Piazzi on his way to third,
and they're going to lead to

00:07:23.303 --> 00:07:25.969
believe, the (inaudible)

00:07:25.969 --> 00:07:28.719
(crowd cheering)

00:07:31.392 --> 00:07:34.012
Derek Jeter, with one of
the most unbelievable plays

00:07:34.012 --> 00:07:36.762
you will ever see by a shortstop.

00:07:39.215 --> 00:07:41.125
- [Interviewer] What's your
message to kids nowadays that

00:07:41.125 --> 00:07:42.577
are playing sports whether
it's baseball or any sport,

00:07:42.577 --> 00:07:45.743
what's your message to the youth of today?

00:07:45.743 --> 00:07:49.948
Have fun playing, you know
I think kids nowadays that

00:07:49.948 --> 00:07:53.370
specialize in one particular
sport at such an early age

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.367
you know when I was younger,
we used to go outside

00:07:56.367 --> 00:07:57.657
and play every game.

00:07:57.657 --> 00:08:00.075
Now you don't really see
kids outside playing.

00:08:00.075 --> 00:08:02.161
They're all inside playing video games.

00:08:02.161 --> 00:08:05.242
Which there's a time and
a place for everything,

00:08:05.242 --> 00:08:08.120
but I say go out and play
as many sports as you can,

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.535
be active and enjoy yourself, have fun.

00:08:11.535 --> 00:08:14.487
You can't lose sight of
the fact that it is a game,

00:08:14.487 --> 00:08:17.320
you're supposed to enjoy yourself.

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:20.133
- [Announcer] Meek deals.

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:21.885
Base hit to left field.

00:08:21.885 --> 00:08:24.181
Here comes Richardson, here's
the throw from Ortegas,

00:08:24.181 --> 00:08:26.377
Richardson is safe!

00:08:26.377 --> 00:08:30.752
Derek Jeter ends his final
game with a walk off single.

00:08:30.752 --> 00:08:34.335
Derek Jeter, where
fantasy becomes reality.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.581
Did you have any doubt?

00:08:38.581 --> 00:08:40.619
- How was Dave Winfield an inspiration?

00:08:40.619 --> 00:08:42.828
Dave was, for those of you
who didn't know, I think he's

00:08:42.828 --> 00:08:46.828
the only athelete to be drafted
and called three sports.

00:08:46.828 --> 00:08:49.246
For anyone here who thinks
they've ever been an athlete,

00:08:49.246 --> 00:08:50.702
you can't compare to Dave.

00:08:50.702 --> 00:08:54.282
But he was an inspiration
maybe because he was one

00:08:54.282 --> 00:08:57.561
of the first athletes to
start his own foundation.

00:08:57.561 --> 00:09:01.769
That's the idea the inspiration
of my own foundation,

00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:04.476
because when I was younger
I read up on Dave, I wanted

00:09:04.476 --> 00:09:05.810
to be like him.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:08.520
(crowd cheering)

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.686
- [Announcer] One two.

00:09:10.686 --> 00:09:13.101
He loops out to left field,
it'll be a tough play,

00:09:13.101 --> 00:09:14.767
Jeter on the run, makes the play.

00:09:14.767 --> 00:09:17.393
And flies into the stands!

00:09:17.393 --> 00:09:19.557
Oh what a play by Derek Jeter!

00:09:19.557 --> 00:09:22.307
(crowd cheering)

00:09:26.881 --> 00:09:30.017
Wow, Jeter really banged himself up.

00:09:30.017 --> 00:09:31.313
- [Commentator] You can see
the redness in his face,

00:09:31.313 --> 00:09:34.521
he caught the bar on that dive.

00:09:34.521 --> 00:09:35.559
What a play!

00:09:35.559 --> 00:09:38.728
- You're going to struggle,
that's the bottom line, I don't

00:09:38.728 --> 00:09:41.936
care how good you are, you're
going to fail I mean you

00:09:41.936 --> 00:09:44.890
fail seven out of 10 times,
you go to the Hall of Fame

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:46.594
you know what I mean.

00:09:46.594 --> 00:09:48.927
I was talking to a group of
my leaders once and this was

00:09:48.927 --> 00:09:51.973
right around the time I got
three thousand hits and I said

00:09:51.973 --> 00:09:54.017
well I have three thousand
hits, but I've gotten out

00:09:54.017 --> 00:09:57.600
seven thousand times,
you know what I mean?

00:09:58.438 --> 00:09:59.522
(laughing)

00:09:59.522 --> 00:10:00.355
It's not funny.

00:10:00.355 --> 00:10:02.605
(laughter)

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:07.736
I don't think it's funny
but you get out quite a bit,

00:10:07.736 --> 00:10:10.395
so I've always just tried to
remember the good times and

00:10:10.395 --> 00:10:15.015
you have to have very short
memory and you have to stay

00:10:15.015 --> 00:10:16.729
positive, but I don't
care what career you're in

00:10:16.729 --> 00:10:18.229
you're going to fail.

00:10:19.182 --> 00:10:22.520
You're going to have tough
times you know, people always

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.920
look at people when they're
successful and they assume

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.085
they just woke up and they
ended up in that position

00:10:29.085 --> 00:10:31.665
but there's a lot of speed
bumps along the way, it's

00:10:31.665 --> 00:10:33.957
just your job to try to
make that speed bump not

00:10:33.957 --> 00:10:35.709
be a road block, you know what I mean?

00:10:35.709 --> 00:10:37.378
But you have to learn from
your mistakes and move on

00:10:37.378 --> 00:10:41.708
and stay positive and mistakes
I think you can learn more,

00:10:41.708 --> 00:10:44.582
I always tend to learn
more from people's failures

00:10:44.582 --> 00:10:46.582
than from their success.

00:10:47.995 --> 00:10:49.757
I think you have that approach.

00:10:49.757 --> 00:10:50.753
(crowd cheering)

00:10:50.753 --> 00:10:54.920
("Empire State of Mind" - JayZ)

